  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An upper ridge centered over the Southeast CONUS and \nsfc high pressure ridging in from the north are keeping ENC dry\nearly this afternoon. PoPs increase late afternoon through\ntonight ahead of and along a front moving through the region. \nWarm and humid will cont through at least early next week as \nPW's rise back above climo.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned cold front is expected to \nmove into and through ENC through Sat. Sfc convergence and \nlowering heights aloft as shortwave energy rides over the upper \nridge along with increasing moisture will allow for a better \nchance of showers and perhaps a few storms late today, and esp \nthis evening. Front slowly sags into srn portions of ENC Sat, \nwith best chances for showers and iso thunderstorms for srn \nareas on Sat. Fcst a bit more muddled beyond Sat as it will \ndepend on the front location, but at this time it appears \ntypical seabreeze showers/storms Sunday into early next week as \nreturn flow develops once again. \n\nKey Message 3...Next week, a strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the\nsame time low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This will likely usher in stronger NE winds to the \nregion. Given it is 4-6 days out, there is still much\nuncertainty, as both wind speed and direction is dependent on\nthe placement, strength, and timing of the low. For now there \nis good model consensus on at least higher waves 8+ ft at 8+\nsec along the Outer Banks mid- next week. This will be worth \nkeeping an eye on, as a long fetch of strong winds could bring \nhigher wave energy towards vulnerable portions of the Outer \nBanks. It is too soon to determine specifics at this point and \ntrends will have to be monitored in the coming days.",
